---
export const lastmod = "2026-07-29";
import AnalyticsOptOut from "@components/AnalyticsOptOut.astro";
import Container from "@components/Container.astro";
import Footer from "@components/Footer.astro";
import Page from "@layouts/Page.astro";

// The app section below is a verbatim copy of box-timer-privacy-policy.md in
// the workout-timer repo. Edit it there first, then bring the change here.
//
// The version is the app's `privacyPolicyVersion`
// (workout-clock/Features/Privacy/BoxTimerAnalytics.swift). Every in-app
// consent record claims the user agreed to a policy carrying it, so the two
// must match: bump this only when the app ships a matching version, never for
// a change to the website section alone.
const effectiveDate = new Date("2026-07-29");
const effectiveDateISO = effectiveDate.toISOString().slice(0, 10);
const effectiveDateLabel = effectiveDate.toLocaleDateString("en-US", {
  year: "numeric",
  month: "long",
  day: "numeric",
  timeZone: "UTC",
});
---

<Page
  title="Box Timer – Privacy Policy"
  description="The privacy policy for the Box Timer app and for boxtimer.app: what each collects, why, on what legal basis, and how to turn analytics off."
  url="https://boxtimer.app/privacy/"
>
  <Container innerClass={"flex-col items-center"}>
    <div class="w-full sm:w-9/12">
      <h1 class="w-full text-4xl lg:text-5xl font-sans font-bold mb-8">
        Privacy Policy
      </h1>
      <p class="pb-8 text-sm text-zinc-600">
        Version <time datetime={effectiveDateISO}>{effectiveDateISO}</time>.
        Effective {effectiveDateLabel}.
      </p>

      <p class="pb-8">
        Box Timer is a workout timer app, and this is its website. This page is
        the privacy policy for both: <a href="#app">the app</a> first, then
        <a href="#website">this website</a>. They collect different things, so
        each is described on its own.
      </p>

      <h2
        id="app"
        class="w-full text-3xl font-sans font-bold mb-8 mt-16 scroll-mt-28"
      >
        The app
      </h2>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-sans font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Who is responsible</h3>
      <p class="pb-8">
        I run Box Timer personally, not as a company, so the person responsible
        for everything here is me, Edvin Lindén. Write to
        <a href="mailto:help@boxtimer.app">help@boxtimer.app</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="pb-8">
        If you email me, your message and address stay in my email archive.
        Nothing goes on a mailing list, nothing is shared, and I'll delete a
        message on request.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-sans font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
        What Box Timer collects
      </h3>
      <p class="pb-8">
        Box Timer collects usage analytics. With analytics switched on, this is
        what is collected:
      </p>
      <p class="pb-8">
        <strong>An identifier for your installation of Box Timer.</strong> It cannot
        be traced back to you, and it cannot be matched against any other app, including
        my own.
      </p>
      <p class="pb-8">
        <strong>When it happened.</strong> An identifier for the current session,
        and the date and time.
      </p>
      <p class="pb-4">
        <strong>What happened.</strong> Every event Box Timer sends:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-outside ml-6 space-y-2 mb-8">
        <li>your first session after installing the app</li>
        <li>a session starting</li>
        <li>changing the display theme, with the theme picked</li>
        <li>answering the offer to reuse settings from a past workout, yes or no</li>
        <li>
          deleting a history entry, renaming one, and pinning or unpinning one
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="pb-8">
        <strong>What the app is running on.</strong> The device model, operating
        system, screen size, language, region and time zone, whether the app is in
        light or dark mode, its version, and whether it came from the App Store or
        from a test build.
      </p>
      <p class="pb-8">
        <strong>How much you use the app.</strong> How many sessions you have had,
        how long they last, and how many days you have opened it.
      </p>
      <p class="pb-8">
        <strong>Technical details of the analytics software</strong>, such as its
        version.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-sans font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
        What Box Timer keeps on your device
      </h3>
      <p class="pb-8">
        Analytics keeps a small record of when you have opened the app, which is
        what the usage counts above are worked out from. Events waiting to be
        sent are held on the device too, until they're delivered. Turning
        analytics off deletes both. If you never turn analytics on, it is never
        written.
      </p>
      <p class="pb-8">
        Box Timer also stores your analytics decision: whether it is on or off,
        when and how you decided, the version of this policy in force at the
        time, and the country of your App Store account. The country decides
        whether you have to be asked for consent, and is never sent anywhere.
      </p>
      <p class="pb-8">
        Your workout history, timer presets and settings are stored on your
        device too. They stay there and are never sent.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-sans font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
        What Box Timer doesn't collect
      </h3>
      <p class="pb-8">
        No name, email or account, because Box Timer has no accounts. No
        advertising identifier, no ad networks. No tracking across other
        companies' apps or websites. No GPS or location services data. Nothing
        about your accessibility settings. Nothing about your workouts: not how
        many, not how long, not how often. That data never leaves your device,
        so I have no way to see it. I do not sell data or share it with
        advertisers or brokers.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-sans font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
        Why Box Timer collects this data
      </h3>
      <p class="pb-4">Three reasons, and no others:</p>
      <ol class="list-decimal list-outside ml-6 mb-8">
        <li>
          Checking a release works, by seeing whether the app is still used
          normally afterwards.
        </li>
        <li>
          Seeing which features people find and use, so I know what to improve.
        </li>
        <li>
          Counting users by country, to decide which languages to translate
          into.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="pb-8">
        In the European Economic Area the legal basis is your consent, Article
        6(1)(a) GDPR. Box Timer asks first, sends nothing until you say yes, and
        you can withdraw any time. Everywhere else it is my legitimate
        interests, Article 6(1)(f), those interests being the three purposes
        above, and analytics is on by default with a switch to turn it off.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-sans font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Opting out</h3>
      <p class="pb-8">
        The switch is under About and then Privacy, in the same place whether
        you were asked for consent or not. Turning it off ends collection
        immediately, deletes anything that had not yet been sent, and deletes
        what analytics had stored on your device. Box Timer works the same with
        analytics off.
      </p>
      <p class="pb-8">
        Turning it back on records a fresh decision against the current policy
        version.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-sans font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
        Where the data goes and how long it stays
      </h3>
      <p class="pb-8">
        Data goes to TelemetryDeck GmbH in Augsburg, Germany, which provides the
        analytics service. Their privacy policy is at
        <a
          href="https://telemetrydeck.com/privacy"
          title="TelemetryDeck - Privacy Policy"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer">https://telemetrydeck.com/privacy</a
        >.
      </p>
      <p class="pb-8">
        TelemetryDeck stores it in the EU and does not transfer it outside the
        EU or the EEA. TelemetryDeck keeps it available for 24 months, and after
        that moves it to cold storage rather than deleting it.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-sans font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Your rights</h3>
      <p class="pb-8">
        You have the right to access your data, correct it, delete it, restrict
        its use, object to its use, receive a copy, and withdraw your consent.
        Email me.
      </p>
      <p class="pb-8">
        The analytics data holds nothing that identifies you, so I can't find
        your records in it. Article 11 GDPR doesn't require me to start
        collecting more about you to make that possible, and I won't. What I can
        do is stop collecting from you, which is what the switch above does.
      </p>
      <p class="pb-8">
        You have the right to lodge a complaint with the Swedish data protection
        authority, Integritetsskyddsmyndigheten (IMY), or with the supervisory
        authority where you live.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-sans font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Changes</h3>
      <p class="pb-8">
        This policy may be updated. Changes are published on this page with a
        new effective date.
      </p>

      <h2
        id="website"
        class="w-full text-3xl font-sans font-bold mb-8 mt-16 scroll-mt-28"
      >
        This website
      </h2>
      <p class="pb-8">
        The person responsible is the same: me, Edvin Lindén, at
        <a href="mailto:help@boxtimer.app">help@boxtimer.app</a>. The website
        collects less than the app, and asks for nothing.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-sans font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
        What this website collects
      </h3>
      <p class="pb-8">
        boxtimer.app uses the same analytics service as the app. When you load a
        page, this is sent:
      </p>
      <p class="pb-8">
        <strong>Which visit it was.</strong> An identifier worked out from your IP
        address, an identifier for this site, what your browser says about itself,
        and a code that changes every day. It cannot be traced back to you, it cannot
        follow you to another website, and it stops matching you when the day ends.
      </p>
      <p class="pb-8">
        <strong>When it happened.</strong> The date and time of the request.
      </p>
      <p class="pb-4">
        <strong>What happened.</strong> Every event this site sends:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-outside ml-6 space-y-2 mb-8">
        <li>loading a page</li>
        <li>clicking a button that goes to the App Store</li>
        <li>
          clicking something that turns out not to do anything, and clicking the
          same spot several times in quick succession — for both, a description
          of what you clicked, which is its kind and the first 40 characters of
          its text, and which page you were on
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="pb-8">
        <strong>Which page.</strong> The address of the page you loaded, including
        anything after the question mark in it, and the address of the page you came
        from if you followed a link.
      </p>
      <p class="pb-8">
        <strong>What you're browsing on.</strong> Your browser and its version, your
        operating system and its version, whether you are on a phone, a tablet or a
        desktop, whether the screen responds to touch, your language, and an approximate
        country and continent. All of it comes either from what your browser tells
        every website it visits, or from your IP address.
      </p>
      <p class="pb-8">
        <strong>Technical details of the analytics software</strong>, such as its
        version.
      </p>
      <p class="pb-8">
        No cookies. Nothing is stored on your device unless you opt out below,
        and that opt-out setting is the only thing this site ever looks for
        there.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-sans font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
        What this website doesn't collect
      </h3>
      <p class="pb-8">
        No name, email or account, because the site has no accounts. No
        advertising identifier, no ad networks. No tracking across other
        companies' apps or websites. No location beyond the approximate country
        above. Your IP address is never stored by the analytics service: it is
        used to work out that country and the daily identifier, and then it is
        gone. Nothing you type anywhere is collected, and nothing on this site
        connects your visit here to your use of the app. I do not sell data or
        share it with advertisers or brokers.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-sans font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
        Why this website collects this data
      </h3>
      <p class="pb-4">Three reasons, and no others:</p>
      <ol class="list-decimal list-outside ml-6 mb-8">
        <li>
          Seeing which pages people find and read, so I know which ones are
          worth keeping and improving.
        </li>
        <li>
          Finding the parts of a page that are broken or confusing, which is
          what the clicks that lead nowhere are for.
        </li>
        <li>Seeing how many people go on to the App Store from here.</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="pb-8">
        Legal basis: Article 6(1)(f) GDPR, legitimate interests in understanding
        website traffic well enough to improve the site. The data is minimal, no
        identifier outlasts the day, nothing is stored on your device unless you
        opt out, and none of it is used to profile you or shared with anyone.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-sans font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Opting out</h3>
      <p class="pb-8">
        Use the switch below and analytics stops for you on this site,
        immediately and on this page. It saves one opt-out setting in your
        browser, so it covers this browser on this device. If your browser sends
        a Global Privacy Control signal, analytics is skipped automatically and
        there is nothing for the switch to do. Everything on the site works the
        same either way.
      </p>
      <AnalyticsOptOut />

      <h3 class="text-xl font-sans font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
        Where the data goes and how long it stays
      </h3>
      <p class="pb-8">
        Data goes to TelemetryDeck GmbH in Augsburg, Germany, the same service
        the app uses, on the same terms: stored in the EU, never transferred
        outside the EU or the EEA, kept available for 24 months and then moved
        to cold storage rather than deleted.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-sans font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Hosting</h3>
      <p class="pb-8">
        The site is served by GitHub Pages, operated by GitHub, Inc. (United
        States). GitHub receives your IP address to deliver the page and records
        it in its own logs for security and abuse prevention. I have no access
        to those logs and no control over how long GitHub keeps them.
      </p>
      <p class="pb-8">
        Legal basis: Article 6(1)(f) GDPR, legitimate interests in running the
        site securely.
      </p>
      <p class="pb-8">
        GitHub adheres to the EU-U.S. Data Privacy Framework and also relies on
        the European Commission's Standard Contractual Clauses. Details:
        <a
          href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement"
          title="GitHub General Privacy Statement"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub General Privacy Statement</a
        >.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-sans font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Your rights</h3>
      <p class="pb-8">
        The rights listed above apply to what this website collects too, and so
        does the limit on them: the analytics data holds nothing that identifies
        you and no identifier that outlasts the day, so I can't find your
        records in it. What I can do is stop collecting from you, which is what
        the switch above does. The complaint route is the same, to
        Integritetsskyddsmyndigheten (IMY) or to the supervisory authority where
        you live.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-sans font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Changes</h3>
      <p class="pb-8">
        This policy may be updated. Changes are published on this page with a
        new effective date. Questions about any of it go to
        <a href="mailto:help@boxtimer.app">help@boxtimer.app</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Container>
  <Footer />
</Page>
